{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}.\n$$\n\nWe see that if we let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $a=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(2).$  \n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{f}^{′}(2)={e}^{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude tha $L={e}^{2}.$",
